    <article class="prose">
      <h2>fridayforwork.com</h2>
      <p>
        A career coach for early-career crew in film, TV and theatre — people
        trying to land their first role in an industry that runs on word of
        mouth and closed networks. Their credits are empty, their last job was
        behind a bar, and every posting asks for the same six things without
        telling you which two actually decide it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Generic AI tools make this worse, not better: they hand you a polished
        CV you can't defend when someone reads it back to you in an interview.
        Friday takes the opposite position —
        <strong>it coaches instead of writing</strong>. It scores your CV
        against how this industry actually hires: how credits get read, why
        crew-list order matters, what a first job in the camera department is
        really asking for. Then it asks questions until you can articulate
        your own experience — which happens to be the exact skill the room
        tests next.
      </p>

      <h2>Under the hood</h2>
      <p>
        I designed, built and shipped the whole thing in a single week using
        AI-assisted development end-to-end — strategy, design system,
        LLM-driven coaching flows and the application layer. The interesting
        part isn't the model; it's the domain data underneath. Patterns of how
        productions actually hire are encoded into the product, so a general
        model stops averaging every industry at once and starts giving
        specific advice.
      </p>
      <p>
        The system remembers: answers you give once follow you into every
        future cover letter, practice interviews know which role you're
        chasing, and logged outcomes — interview, offer, rejection, silence —
        feed back into what Friday suggests next. The coaching compounds.
      </p>

      <h2>Why it matters</h2>
      <p>
        Friday is in private beta with crew who are using it to land their
        first roles, tell the story of their own work, and walk into rooms
        able to defend every line on the page. Self-representation is a skill,
        not a document — and it's the skill that decides who gets a chance.
      </p>
